# Ruflo auto-activation (Cursor)

Ruflo Cursor is opt-in. Do not enable it because a task is complex, large, or parallelizable. Enable it only when the user explicitly asks, or when it is already ON.

Explicit ON intent (English, Bulgarian, or equivalent): "Use Ruflo", "Use Ruflo for this task", "Use Ruflo orchestration", "Run this with Ruflo", "Пусни Ruflo", "Използвай Ruflo", "Използвай Ruflo orchestration".

Explicit OFF intent: "Turn Ruflo off", "Disable Ruflo", "Stop using Ruflo", "Спри Ruflo", "Изключи Ruflo", "Продължи без Ruflo".

## Explicit ON

1. Run `ruflo-cursor-status` from this project.
2. If OFF, run `ruflo-cursor-on`. If already ON, do not re-run enable/setup.
3. Run `ruflo-cursor-status` and confirm ON.
4. Detect whether Ruflo MCP is loaded in THIS chat: the live tool catalog must include a Ruflo MCP server/tools (search namespaces/tools for `ruflo`). `.cursor/mcp.json` on disk is not enough.
5. If Ruflo is ON but this chat has no Ruflo MCP tools, MCP changed after the chat started. Tell the user clearly: Ruflo is now ON, but Cursor requires a **new Agent chat** to load the MCP. Stop. Do not pretend tools exist. Do not start the Ruflo-orchestrated task in this stale chat.
6. If Ruflo MCP tools are already available here, continue immediately.

## Explicit OFF

Do not disable Ruflo after a task. Leave it ON unless the user explicitly asks to disable it.

On explicit OFF:

1. `ruflo-cursor-stop`
2. `ruflo-cursor-off`
3. `ruflo-cursor-status` and confirm OFF
4. If this chat still shows Ruflo MCP tools from the old session, tell the user to open a new Agent chat for a clean non-Ruflo context.

## Execution (only when ON and loaded, or after a new chat)

Use the existing Cursor adapter `.ruflo/cursor/bin/adapter.mjs` with real Cursor Agent CLI workers. Never `codex exec`. Never consume Codex quota. Use `.ruflo/cursor/model-routing.json`. Parallel workers only when useful. Do not reinstall Ruflo, do not modify the Codex Ruflo integration, `~/.codex`, or global Cursor MCP.
